  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled weather expected through midweek as a \nfront remains stalled over the region...temperatures trending \ncooler by Wednesday.\n\nWarm front lifts north of us today, with frontal boundary\nstalling along the Mason-Dixon line tonight. Models have SBCAPEs\nof 1500-2000 J/kg this afternoon and could see a few strong\nstorms as forecast soundings indicate some drier air in the low\nlevels, so cannot rule out an isolated downburst. High res \nmodel solutions seem to be concentrating the best coverage this \nafternoon along/near the Blue Ridge Sunday, so will have higher \npops here. \n\n\nFront tracks into our area Monday and pretty much meanders\nbetween the Carolinas and southern Mid-Atlantic into midweek,\nthen the upper trough forces it southeast after Wednesday as it\ndigs in from the northwest. Rainfall amounts from today through \nWednesday should average around 1-2\" per ensembles, with locally\nhigher amounts under any storms. This is much needed rainfall, \nbut if too much falls in short amounts of time, could see some \nlocalized flooding concerns especially Monday-Tuesday.\n\nCooler air from the north with high pressure wedge will have \nour highs Wednesday running 10 to 15 degrees below normal, \nfollowed by a gradual return to near normal temperatures by the \nweekend.",
